STATEMENT 3: InsertProductAsync - Multi-Statement Transaction
================================================================================
DMS Conversion Attempt: 2026-01-27T12:01:52
DMS Status: ERROR
DMS Error: Metadata model creation failed: {'error': "Metadata model creation failed: {'default_error_details': {'message': 'Statement definition is not valid.'}}"}

Original SQL Statement:
-----------------------
DECLARE @NewProductId INT;

BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
    VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity);
    
    SET @NewProductId = SCOPE_IDENTITY();
    
    INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
    VALUES (@NewProductId, 'INSERT', NULL, @Price, NULL, @StockQuantity, GETDATE());
    
    UPDATE ProductStats
    SET 
        TotalProducts = TotalProducts + 1,
        AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts + @Price) / (TotalProducts + 1),
        LastUpdated = GETDATE()
    WHERE StatId = 1;
COMMIT;

SELECT @NewProductId;

Manual Conversion Applied:
---------------------------
Reasoning: Major PostgreSQL syntax differences:
1. DECLARE not needed - variables handled differently in PostgreSQL functions/procedures
2. SCOPE_IDENTITY() → Use RETURNING clause in INSERT to get new ID
3. GETDATE() → NOW() or CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
4. BEGIN TRANSACTION; COMMIT; → PostgreSQL transactions managed at application level
5. SET variable → Variables used differently in PostgreSQL stored procedures

For ADO.NET application code (not stored procedure), PostgreSQL approach:
- Remove DECLARE and variable management (handled in application code)
- Use INSERT...RETURNING to get new ID directly
- Replace GETDATE() with NOW()
- Transaction control handled by NpgsqlConnection.BeginTransaction() in application

PostgreSQL Converted Statement:
INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity)
RETURNING ProductId;

INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
VALUES (currval('products_productid_seq'), 'INSERT', NULL, @Price, NULL, @StockQuantity, NOW());

UPDATE ProductStats
SET 
    TotalProducts = TotalProducts + 1,
    AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts + @Price) / (TotalProducts + 1),
    LastUpdated = NOW()
WHERE StatId = 1;

Conversion Method: MANUAL_AFTER_DMS_FAILURE
Changes Applied:
  - Restructured INSERT to use RETURNING clause for new ID
  - Replaced SCOPE_IDENTITY() with currval('products_productid_seq')
  - Replaced GETDATE() with NOW()
  - Removed DECLARE, SET, BEGIN TRANSACTION, COMMIT (handled at application level)
  - Split into separate statements for application-level transaction control

