Manual Conversion Applied:
---------------------------
Reasoning: In ADO.NET application context, variable management moved to application code.
PostgreSQL requires restructuring the transaction block to use WITH clauses or separate queries.
For ADO.NET, the pattern is to execute multiple queries within application transaction.

PostgreSQL Converted Statements (executed within application transaction):
WITH OldValues AS (
    SELECT Price as OldPrice, StockQuantity as OldStock
    FROM Products
    WHERE ProductId = @ProductId
)
UPDATE Products
SET 
    Name = @Name,
    Description = @Description,
    Price = @Price,
    StockQuantity = @StockQuantity,
    ModifiedDate = NOW()
WHERE ProductId = @ProductId
RETURNING (SELECT OldPrice FROM OldValues) as OldPrice, (SELECT OldStock FROM OldValues) as OldStock;

-- Or split into separate statements for application code:
-- Query 1: Get old values
SELECT Price as OldPrice, StockQuantity as OldStock
FROM Products
WHERE ProductId = @ProductId;

-- Query 2: Update product
UPDATE Products
SET 
    Name = @Name,
    Description = @Description,
    Price = @Price,
    StockQuantity = @StockQuantity,
    ModifiedDate = NOW()
WHERE ProductId = @ProductId;

-- Query 3: Insert history
INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
VALUES (@ProductId, 'UPDATE', @OldPrice, @Price, @OldStock, @StockQuantity, NOW());

-- Query 4: Update stats
UPDATE ProductStats
SET 
    AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts - @OldPrice + @Price) / TotalProducts,
    LastUpdated = NOW()
WHERE StatId = 1;

